  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Replace each letter with its value in brackets, then use BIDMAS.",
      "A negative number squared is positive.",
      "To change the subject, undo operations on both sides in reverse order.",
      "If the new subject appears twice, collect those terms, factorise, then divide."
    ],
    "title": "Substituting and Rearranging Formulae",
    "content": "<p><strong>Substitution</strong> means replacing letters with numbers and working the result out with BIDMAS: brackets and powers before × and ÷, and those before + and −.</p><p><strong>Rearranging</strong> a formula changes which letter is the subject. Undo each operation on both sides in reverse order, so a + becomes a −, a × becomes a ÷, and a power becomes a root.</p><p>When the new subject appears more than once, gather its terms on one side, factorise it out, then divide.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Given</strong> \\(v = u + at\\), find \\(v\\) when \\(u = 5\\), \\(a = 3\\), \\(t = 4\\).</p><p>\\(v = 5 + 3 \\times 4 = 5 + 12 = 17\\)</p>"
  },
